@carlosparadis I had to wrap up a project for another class as well as take care of other homework that had immediate deadlines.

I started the re-do of the `smell_detect_ragequit` function in [the commit referenced above](https://github.com/usradam/kaiaulu/commit/fdcec2d4b828bdc07c2c35a5334e41738db7ea6b). The function should now take care of three steps as outlined [here](https://github.com/sailuh/kaiaulu/issues/360#issuecomment-3902355487). I also compiled the package and documentation according to the contributing guidelines stated [here](https://github.com/sailuh/kaiaulu?tab=contributing-ov-file#). 

I will start work on the new `.Rmd` file in the `/vignettes` folder to test the function with the CSV data I generated from the outline above.

@usradam 

1. Yes, the `created_at` column uses ISO 8601 datetime format with timezone: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`
2. Yes, commit comments will have the exact same column names, data types, and format as PR comments. The only difference will be the context (commit vs PR), but the schema is identical.

@cfuke1 I am glad you sorted out covr, but be careful not get stuck in the details: It is more important that you have broader coverage across the different notebooks than end the project with a single function tested to infinity. 

From covr, try to get information associated to what unit tests were tied to the functions for now. I find it hard that codeconv does not offer any tangible benefit over covr, otherwise their company would have gone bankrupt. One thing that I've seen from codecov is the ability for it to pop up on a pull request and do some checks in the code review process. You should account for that as value and as well shareable links of codecov when going through this.

If you think about it, the GitHub Actions are also running unit tests you can run locally and running r:cmdcheck that can also be run locally. But there is still added value on having that CI/CD pipeline.

@john-a-flinn I recommend you do an example for this:

https://github.com/sailuh/kaiaulu/issues/358#issuecomment-3978516342

To make sure we are on the same page. Take one common table and show me how some functions that it uses would look like with the overload.

@john-a-flinn slow down a bit: The code is not formatted in the comment properly, the code blocks are missing the highlight, you jumped straight to R6, one of the OO systems (which actually is not the one Kaiaulu uses) without even confirming which one made the most sense (and arguing for it). 

I am also not seeing any rationale on where you got functions from, etc. 

Please take some time thinking this through, edit the comment above then follow-up.
